From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: android-kitkat-tls12 <android-kitkat-tls12@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 27/32] Do not offer a cached client session of a lower
 protocol version

OpenSSL 1.0.1's SSL_set_session switches the SSL to the protocol
version of the session it is given (ssl/ssl_sess.c, SSL_set_session ->
SSL_set_ssl_method). OpenSSLSocketImpl.getCachedClientSession offered a
cached session whenever its protocol was enabled at all, so a session
cached over TLSv1 (by KitKat before the TLS 1.2 defaults of libcore/0006 and
persisted by SSLSessionCache, e.g. SSLCertificateSocketFactory and
AndroidHttpClient, or made once by a socket with only TLSv1 enabled)
made the next ClientHello a TLSv1 one. If the server resumed it, the
connection stayed on TLSv1; if it never resumes, every full handshake
cached the next TLSv1 session, so the host stayed on TLSv1 for good.

getCachedClientSession now also skips a session whose protocol is
below the highest enabled protocol. A socket whose enabled protocols
stop at the session's version still resumes it; that includes okhttp's
fallback retry (TLSv1 + TLS_FALLBACK_SCSV), which enables only TLSv1.
Cost: a socket with the TLS 1.2 defaults against a server that only
speaks TLSv1 makes a full handshake each time instead of resuming.

Test: SSLSocketTest.test_SSLSocket_sessionReuse_lowerProtocolNotOffered
(TLSv1 session cached by a {TLSv1} client of the same context and
resumed by the next {TLSv1} client; a client with the defaults then
negotiates TLSv1.2 with a new session, which is resumed next). Before
this change the default client resumed the TLSv1 session.

Android 4.1 port (android-4.1.2_r2) of patch 0028 of the 4.4 series
(renumbered: the 4.4 patch 0025, the ssl.SocketFactory.provider hook of
the TLS 1.3 layer, is not part of this series), made from its Android
4.2 port. Paths and names: libcore/crypto of 4.4 (org.conscrypt,
org_conscrypt_NativeCrypto.cpp in libjavacrypto) is libcore/luni on 4.1
(org.apache.harmony.xnet.provider.jsse,
org_apache_harmony_xnet_provider_jsse_NativeCrypto.cpp in libjavacore).
Otherwise the same change; only line offsets differ.
---
 .../xnet/provider/jsse/OpenSSLSocketImpl.java | 35 +++++++++++++++++
 .../libcore/javax/net/ssl/SSLSocketTest.java  | 38 +++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 73 insertions(+)

diff --git a/luni/src/main/java/org/apache/harmony/xnet/provider/jsse/OpenSSLSocketImpl.java b/luni/src/main/java/org/apache/harmony/xnet/provider/jsse/OpenSSLSocketImpl.java
index e8b510cacdd..c41043fad8b 100644
--- a/luni/src/main/java/org/apache/harmony/xnet/provider/jsse/OpenSSLSocketImpl.java
+++ b/luni/src/main/java/org/apache/harmony/xnet/provider/jsse/OpenSSLSocketImpl.java
@@ -225,6 +225,16 @@ public class OpenSSLSocketImpl
         if (!protocolFound) {
             return null;
         }
+        // kktls: never offer a session of a lower protocol version than the highest enabled one.
+        // SSL_set_session switches the SSL to the session's version, so a session cached over
+        // TLSv1 (by 4.4 before the TLS 1.2 defaults, persisted by SSLSessionCache, or made by an
+        // app that enabled only TLSv1 once) would pin this host to TLSv1 for as long as the
+        // server does not resume it: each full handshake would create the next TLSv1 session.
+        // A fallback retry (okhttp: TLSv1 + TLS_FALLBACK_SCSV) enables only its lower version,
+        // so it still resumes its own sessions.
+        if (protocolVersion(protocol) < highestProtocolVersion(enabledProtocols)) {
+            return null;
+        }
 
         String cipherSuite = session.getCipherSuite();
         boolean cipherSuiteFound = false;
@@ -255,6 +265,31 @@ public class OpenSSLSocketImpl
         return session;
     }
 
+    /**
+     * kktls: the order of the protocol names that NativeCrypto supports (SSLv3 &lt; TLSv1 &lt;
+     * TLSv1.1 &lt; TLSv1.2); -1 for any other name.
+     */
+    private static int protocolVersion(String protocol) {
+        if ("SSLv3".equals(protocol)) {
+            return 0;
+        } else if ("TLSv1".equals(protocol)) {
+            return 1;
+        } else if ("TLSv1.1".equals(protocol)) {
+            return 2;
+        } else if ("TLSv1.2".equals(protocol)) {
+            return 3;
+        }
+        return -1;
+    }
+
+    private static int highestProtocolVersion(String[] protocols) {
+        int highest = -1;
+        for (String protocol : protocols) {
+            highest = Math.max(highest, protocolVersion(protocol));
+        }
+        return highest;
+    }
+
